Poll — push is off the table here. `PLATFORM.md` rules it out explicitly: WebSocket upgrades 501 at the edge, functions are frozen between invocations (no lingering SSE stream), and there's no sticky routing to keep a connection pinned to one instance anyway.

**Build it as:** `dashboard.js` keeps calling `/api/metrics` (unchanged) on a `setInterval`, but only re-renders when the returned `computed_at` differs from the last one it saw:

```js
let lastComputedAt = null;
async function poll() {
  const res = await fetch('/api/metrics');
  const { rows, computed_at } = await res.json();
  if (computed_at !== lastComputedAt) {
    lastComputedAt = computed_at;
    render(rows);
    document.getElementById('stamp').textContent = new Date(computed_at).toLocaleTimeString();
  }
}
window.addEventListener('load', poll);
setInterval(poll, 15000);
```

No new endpoint, no server change — `rollup.md` says this table only moves once a minute, so a 15–20s client interval catches every update with a few seconds of latency, at 4KB/30ms per hit that's negligible load.

The one real fork: fixed-interval polling (above) vs. schedule-aware polling that sleeps until just after the next `:00` boundary. Schedule-aware is fewer requests but couples the client to the finance-close cron timing, which the doc says isn't ours to shorten and could drift; fixed-interval is dumber but immune to that coupling. I'd default to fixed-interval.

Left out: pausing the interval via the Page Visibility API when the tab isn't focused, and backoff on fetch failure — both are next-level-down polish, not architecture.
